<script server>
import report from '~/public/benchmarks/results.json'
const title = 'The declaration benchmark lab'
const description = 'Compare dtsx with TypeScript, Oxc, SWC, zig-dtsx, tsdown, tsup, Rollup, API Extractor, and unplugin tools using reproducible benchmarks.'
const pathname = '/benchmarks/'
const measuredDate = new Date(report.measuredAt).toLocaleDateString('en-US', { year: 'numeric', month: 'long', day: 'numeric', timeZone: 'America/Los_Angeles' })
const platformLabel = report.machine.platform === 'darwin' ? 'macOS' : report.machine.platform
const memoryGiB = Math.round(report.machine.memoryBytes / (1024 ** 3))
const environmentNote = report.executionEnvironment?.kind === 'github-actions' ? 'This snapshot uses a hosted GitHub Actions runner. Virtualization and shared host activity can affect timings.' : 'This snapshot uses a developer machine with uncontrolled background activity.'
const groups = [
  { id: 'project-emit', name: 'Per-module emission', detail: 'Declarations per source file. A semantic typecheck is not required.' },
  { id: 'checked-project', name: 'Checked compiler builds', detail: 'Includes semantic diagnostics. These compilers do extra work; compare within this group.' },
  { id: 'bundled-project', name: 'Standalone bundles', detail: 'One public declaration entry. Checking and JavaScript work vary by pipeline.' },
]
const sizes = [1, 25, 100]
const measured = report.results.filter(row => row.status === 'passed').length
const fastestEmitter = report.results.filter(row => row.modules === 100 && row.group === 'project-emit' && row.status === 'passed').sort((a, b) => a.medianMs - b.medianMs)[0].name
const fastestBundler = report.results.filter(row => row.modules === 100 && row.group === 'bundled-project' && row.status === 'passed').sort((a, b) => a.medianMs - b.medianMs)[0].name
</script>
<script client>
const theme = useColorMode({ attribute: 'data-theme' })
const size = state('100')
</script>
@nolayout
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>@include('head')</head>
<body>
@include('nav')
<main id="main" class="document-main shell">
  <a class="back-link" href="/">← Back to dtsx</a>
  <div class="document-heading"><h1>The numbers.<br><span>And how we got them.</span></h1><p>A fair comparison starts with a clear workload. Pick a project size and compare tools doing similar work.</p></div>
  <div class="lab-meta"><div><strong>16</strong><span>tool configurations</span></div><div><strong>3</strong><span>project sizes</span></div><div><strong>9</strong><span>samples per case</span></div><div><strong>{{ measured }}/48</strong><span>qualified cases</span></div></div>
  <div class="lab-controls"><label for="project-size">Library size</label><select id="project-size" x-model="size"><option value="1">1 module + entry</option><option value="25">25 modules + entry</option><option value="100">100 modules + entry</option></select><a class="text-link" href="/benchmarks/results.json" download>Download raw results ↓</a></div>
  @foreach(groups as group)
  <section class="lab-group" aria-labelledby="group-{{ group.id }}"><h2 id="group-{{ group.id }}">{{ group.name }}</h2><p>{{ group.detail }}</p>
    @foreach(sizes as count)
    <div :if="size() === '{{ count }}'" class="table-scroll"><table class="results-table"><caption>{{ count }} {{ count === 1 ? 'module' : 'modules' }} + one entrypoint · milliseconds · lower is faster</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Tool / version</th><th scope="col">Median</th><th scope="col">Min - max</th><th scope="col">Samples</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    @foreach(report.results.filter(row => row.group === group.id && row.modules === count).sort((a, b) => (a.medianMs ?? Infinity) - (b.medianMs ?? Infinity)) as row)
    <tr class="{{ row.id.startsWith('dtsx') ? 'highlight-row' : '' }}"><th scope="row">{{ row.name }}<small>{{ row.version }}</small></th>@if(row.status === 'passed')<td class="number">{{ row.medianMs.toFixed(1) }}</td><td class="number range">{{ row.minMs.toFixed(1) }} - {{ row.maxMs.toFixed(1) }}</td><td>{{ row.samplesMs.length }} / 9</td>@else<td colspan="3" class="unavailable">Failed qualification; see the raw results for details</td>@endif</tr>
    @endforeach
    </tbody></table></div>
    @endforeach
  </section>
  @endforeach
  <noscript><p class="notice">Download the raw results for every project size. Enable JavaScript to filter the tables.</p></noscript>
  <section id="methodology" class="methodology"><h2>What these results measure.</h2><div class="method-grid"><article><h3>Same source. Fresh process.</h3><p>Each tool receives the same synthetic, annotated library. Every sample starts in a new process and a fresh output directory. Filesystem caches are warm. Incremental and result caches are not reused.</p></article><article><h3>Useful output comes first.</h3><p>Every sample must exit successfully, emit declarations, and pass a strict consumer covering every module. Two negative assignments must produce exactly TS2322. Bundled declarations are tested in a standalone directory.</p></article><article><h3>The whole command counts.</h3><p>Wall time includes startup, package loading, source reads, emission, writes, and disposal. Cleanup and correctness checks happen outside timing. Two warmups precede nine samples, with tool order rotated each round.</p></article><article><h3>Different tools do different work.</h3><p>TypeScript compilers perform semantic checks. API Extractor and Rollup include prerequisite tsc emission. Vite runs its JavaScript pipeline. tsup and tsdown use declaration-only mode. The adapter source shows every setting.</p></article></div>
  <div class="notice"><h3>One machine. A defined workload.</h3><p>Measured {{ measuredDate }} on {{ report.machine.cpu }}, {{ platformLabel }} {{ report.machine.arch }}, {{ report.machine.cores }} CPU cores, {{ memoryGiB }} GiB memory, {{ report.runtime }}. {{ environmentNote }} Ranges and raw samples are visible.</p><p>The fixtures exercise annotated interfaces, re-exports, generics, private class fields, mapped types, and enums. They exclude external dependencies, JSX, watch mode, declaration maps, and incremental rebuilds. The checks qualify this API; they do not prove correctness for arbitrary TypeScript.</p><p>Zig uses the official <a href="{{ report.nativeZig.url }}">zig-dtsx {{ report.nativeZig.version }} release</a>, with its matching Bun FFI binding and platform library. The setup verifies pinned SHA-256 checksums before measuring. The source commit and artifact checksums are included in the raw results. No development build is substituted.</p></div>
  <p>For the 100-module workload, {{ fastestEmitter }} has the lowest per-module emission median and {{ fastestBundler }} has the lowest standalone bundle median among these configurations. Close medians with overlapping ranges should be treated as similar performance. Use the workload that matches your project, and rerun before choosing a tool on performance alone.</p>
  <p>The separate Ubuntu run could not load the official Linux Zig library. Its raw snapshot records those failed qualifications with no Zig timings. The macOS comparison above includes Zig at every size.</p>
  <div class="document-links"><a href="https://github.com/stacksjs/dtsx.org/tree/main/benchmarks">Inspect and rerun the suite ↗</a><a href="/benchmarks/methodology.md" download>Download the methodology ↓</a><a href="/benchmarks/results-linux.json" download>Download the Linux qualification snapshot ↓</a><a href="https://github.com/stacksjs/dtsx.org/actions/workflows/benchmarks.yml">Run the macOS / Linux workflow ↗</a></div>
  </section>
</main>
@include('footer')
</body>
</html>
